# Response headers applied at the edge.
#
# The CSP is deliberately near-total: both pages are self-contained HTML with an
# inline <style> block and load nothing from anywhere else — no scripts, no fonts,
# no analytics. `default-src 'none'` is therefore accurate rather than aspirational,
# and it should FAIL LOUDLY if anyone later adds a tracker or a webfont to a privacy
# policy page, which is exactly the point.
#
# Two narrow allowances, and only two:
#   style-src 'unsafe-inline' — the CSS is inlined in each page.
#   img-src 'self'            — the logo, served from this origin. Deliberately NOT
#                               a wildcard: a remote image is a tracking pixel by
#                               another name, and on a privacy policy that would be
#                               self-refuting.
#
# Outbound links (Google, Apple, CNIL) are navigations, not resource loads, so they
# are unaffected by any of this.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), interest-cohort=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
